MULTISYNTH TODO

1. FINISH THE FULL REAL-MIDI MODULE REBUILD

This is the only active project-level TODO.

TOP PRIORITY — FIRST-CLASS MIDI PATCHING
- MIDI must be a first-class node-graph signal alongside Carrier/audio and clock.
- Every module that consumes MIDI exposes a visible MIDI IN jack on its node console.
- Every module that generates MIDI exposes a visible MIDI OUT jack.
- Modules that both consume and generate MIDI expose both.
- MIDI cables only connect MIDI OUT to MIDI IN; they cannot cross Carrier or clock jacks.
- MIDI connections persist with the patch exactly like audio and clock connections.
- MIDI generated by a module travels only through explicitly patched MIDI routes unless a deliberately defined hardware/global bridge says otherwise.
- Onboard/local keyboards remain scoped to their owning module unless that module explicitly generates MIDI OUT.
- Control Freak is the main real-hardware MIDI liaison/controller surface: hardware MIDI enters through Control Freak, its mapped controls become normal MIDI, and its MIDI OUT can be patched to any MIDI-consuming module.
- Been Served receives its Note On/Off, velocity, CC64 sustain and mapped envelope control through MIDI IN; Carrier remains a separate audio input.
- This MIDI-jack foundation is completed before continuing broad module-by-module MIDI rebuild work.

Goal
- Finish rebuilding every registered production module plus the approved planned Gene Sequencer against the current real-MIDI architecture and canonical control library.
- `app/src/main/assets/module-manifest.js` is the authoritative active registered module roster.
- `modules.md` is the musician-facing rebuild contract and status ledger for the registered roster plus approved planned modules.
- Reuse established software patterns wherever applicable: state shape, MIDI/event flow, timing/scheduling, lifecycle handling, persistence, editor/runtime separation, canonical controls/prefabs, and smoke coverage.
- Diverge only for genuine module-specific behavior.
- Do not restore retired ladder synths or compatibility paths. Hook and Ladder is the active expandable operator-ladder instrument.
- No fake CV/private MIDI-like substitute where real MIDI already defines the message.
- No bespoke replacements for canonical controls.

PureSynth reference-source rule
- PureSynth is a continuously running reference oscillator, not a conventional envelope-gated synth voice.
- Its pitch encoder and keyboard both write the same oscillator frequency state; they do not combine.
- Key release does not stop the oscillator.
- PureSynth has no local ADSR or sustain/persist mechanism. Downstream modules such as Been Served provide articulation when desired.

Completion gate for each module
- exact musician-facing behavior agreed
- real MIDI mappings complete
- required MIDI IN/OUT jacks exposed and routable
- UI and external MIDI address the same module state
- timing uses the shared transport where applicable
- persistence/restore and lifecycle cleanup work
- actual behavior has relevant smoke coverage
- full CI/build is green
- `modules.md` status updated

